WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.620
<v Riley>Hey there — on today's episode, a $1.2 billion gold mine construction greenlight for the Cariboo, plus a USDA rancher rescue plan that one producer already called "a nice fluff piece." And our Deep Dive asks: when a program's written for ranchers, forests, or Indigenous land, who's actually holding the pen? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.095 --> 00:01:00.740
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Tuesday, September 15th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry.

00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:11.996
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Forestry, ranching, mining, farming — today's about who actually captures the value when those industries change, and who gets left holding the bill.

00:01:12.274 --> 00:01:33.727
<v Casey>Quick weather check — overcast around 9 degrees out at Horsefly Lake this morning, high of 19 expected later, about a third chance of rain. Around the region: 100 Mile House sitting at 8 and overcast, Williams Lake at 10 with a light drizzle, Quesnel at 8 and cloudy, Redstone at 7, also overcast.

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:35.595
<v Riley>Let's get into it.

00:01:36.995 --> 00:02:00.460
<v Riley>Nine stories close to home today, three more on the theme, and a couple worth a quick mention after that. It's a full slate, so let's move. Election filings are stacking up across the region, and the numbers tell you something about who wants a hand in local government right now. Fourteen candidates have filed for School District 27 trustee seats across the Cariboo-Chilcotin.

00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:06.217
<v Casey>Fourteen people running for a board most residents couldn't name a single sitting trustee on.

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:20.834
<v Riley>In 100 Mile House, the field is smaller but still contested — ten candidates running for council, two for mayor. The Free Press notes the candidates are coming from across the South Cariboo, not just the town itself.

00:02:21.518 --> 00:02:35.085
<v Casey>Meanwhile in Williams Lake, a familiar name just re-entered the race. Walt Cobb wants his old job back — a fourth term as mayor, after already serving from 1990 to '96 and again from 2014 to 2022.

00:02:35.341 --> 00:02:41.941
<v Riley>Thirty-plus years on and off in that chair. That's institutional memory nobody else in the race can match.

00:02:42.198 --> 00:02:53.564
<v Casey>Whether that's an asset or a problem depends on whether voters want continuity or a clean break. He's running against a South Cariboo realtor and at least one other declared candidate.

00:02:54.248 --> 00:03:10.381
<v Riley>Quesnel's field is bigger again — three candidates for mayor, eighteen for council. The Cariboo Observer points out every sitting incumbent in both races is seeking re-election, which means voters get a genuine choice between the known quantity and the newcomers, seat by seat.

00:03:11.066 --> 00:03:36.365
<v Casey>Provincially, the legislature keeps reshuffling. Donegal Wilson has become the seventh MLA to leave the B.C. Conservatives for the new party being led by Peter Milobar. That resets the standings again — the NDP holds 47 seats, the Conservatives are down to 28, Milobar's new party is up to seven, the Greens hold two, there are seven independents — all former Conservative MLAs — and two seats currently empty.

00:03:36.621 --> 00:03:43.954
<v Riley>Seven defections in a matter of months. That's not a caucus shedding a member here and there, that's an exodus.

00:03:44.211 --> 00:04:00.710
<v Casey>Closer to street level now — Quesnel's fire department had a busy weekend. Two kitchen fires in downtown apartment buildings on Sunday, September 13th. Fire Chief Booker says no one was hurt in either call, and crews kept both fires contained to the kitchens themselves.

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:18.603
<v Riley>On the industrial side, and this one's a big number — Osisko Gold Group is moving ahead with construction of the Cariboo underground gold mine, a $1.2 billion U.S. build in central B.C. That's roughly 841 million U.S. dollars, targeting first gold production once construction wraps.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:27.659
<v Casey>That's one of the largest single capital commitments announced in this region in years, and it comes after years of permitting and feasibility work.

00:04:27.916 --> 00:04:36.349
<v Riley>It's a real employer for Wells and Quesnel if the timeline holds — construction jobs first, then operational jobs once the mine's running.

00:04:36.606 --> 00:04:50.905
<v Casey>If the timeline holds. Big underground builds have a way of finding new delays once the shovels are actually in the ground — geotechnical surprises, labor shortages, commodity price swings that change the whole economics of the mine mid-construction.

00:04:51.162 --> 00:04:59.962
<v Riley>A formal construction greenlight from the board is a different signal than a press release about "advancing feasibility studies," though. Money's actually moving now.

00:05:00.646 --> 00:05:17.879
<v Casey>There's also a resolution on something a little sweeter than gold — the Staccato cherry dispute has settled. It's a variety developed in Summerland, B.C., that ended up at the center of an international ownership fight over who had the right to grow and sell it commercially.

00:05:18.135 --> 00:05:30.602
<v Riley>Plant breeders' rights disputes almost never make headlines outside the agriculture press, but they decide who profits off decades of orchard research and who gets shut out of a variety they invested years into.

00:05:31.628 --> 00:05:57.294
<v Casey>From B.C. orchards to South African townships now — Johannesburg's weigh-and-pay shops are tackling what organizers call the "poverty tax," the extra cost people pay for buying food and household goods in small quantities because they can't afford to buy in bulk. Six shops, run out of shipping containers across Alexandra township, sell staples like sugar and cooking oil by weight, in whatever small amount someone can afford that day.

00:05:57.551 --> 00:06:06.350
<v Riley>That's a supply chain built around what people can actually afford on a given morning, not around what's cheapest to stock on a shelf.

00:06:06.607 --> 00:06:18.706
<v Casey>Something a food security group here might look at if they ever want to test a bulk-buying co-op model for isolated Cariboo communities where the nearest big-box grocery store is an hour's drive.

00:06:19.562 --> 00:06:33.495
<v Riley>Engineering has its own version of adapting to hard constraints. Researchers have taught a six-legged robot to walk the way a stick insect does — using trial-and-error learning to develop its gait rather than pre-programming every joint movement.

00:06:33.751 --> 00:06:49.884
<v Casey>The pitch is disaster response — reaching places wheeled or tracked vehicles can't, like earthquake rubble or terrain too uneven for a standard rescue robot. Neat, until you remember most volunteer search-and-rescue groups around here can't afford a drone, let alone a robot insect.

00:06:50.569 --> 00:07:07.801
<v Riley>And a financial settlement that's been years in the making — Abbott has agreed to pay $385 million to settle claims tied to the closure of its largest baby formula plant in the U.S., the same plant whose shutdown triggered the nationwide formula shortage back in 2022.

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:28.994
<v Casey>Two quick ones to round out the close-to-home and general science stories. Researchers have finished mapping the complete central nervous system — the full wiring diagram, or connectome — of both male and female fruit flies. And they're already putting that map to unusual use: running trading algorithms and simple games off simulated fly-brain circuitry.

00:07:29.251 --> 00:07:36.584
<v Riley>A fly brain picking stocks. There's a joke about hedge fund managers in there I'm just going to leave alone.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:56.640
<v Casey>Ha. Fair. And on the health side — researchers are finding that gut symptoms like constipation, difficulty swallowing, and irritable bowel issues without diarrhea, appearing decades before diagnosis, might be some of the earliest measurable signals of Parkinson's disease. The study calls for more screening of these gastrointestinal symptoms in people at higher risk.

00:07:57.324 --> 00:08:06.857
<v Riley>Decades of warning signs sitting in plain sight before anyone connects them to the disease. That's worth more attention from primary care doctors, not just specialists.

00:08:07.884 --> 00:08:29.516
<v Casey>Last story today, and it's not ours to vote on, but it's worth tracking. A new Department of Homeland Security report has confirmed what detainees at the Florida detention camp known as Alligator Alcatraz were saying for months — that people were routinely held in small metal enclosures with as little as 18 square feet of floor space each.

00:08:29.773 --> 00:08:43.339
<v Riley>Eighteen square feet. That's smaller than most bathrooms, and it's a pure U.S. policy story with no vote for anyone listening here in the Cariboo — but detention infrastructure ideas don't always stay south of the border.

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:49.157
<v Riley>That's the roundup. Let's talk about who's actually keeping this region's food economy running.

00:08:50.557 --> 00:09:19.921
<v Casey>Today's spotlight is on Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation, an Indigenous joint venture working across Tŝideldel, Tl'etinqox, and Yunesit'in territories. They take fire-damaged and beetle-killed timber and turn restoration into an actual operating business — not a one-off grant project that dries up after the press release. It's Nation-owned, it employs people locally, and it's been running long enough across multiple fire seasons to prove the model holds up when nobody's watching.

00:09:20.214 --> 00:09:34.477
<v Riley>We've mentioned them before on this show, and every time it's for the same reason — ownership actually sitting with the community doing the labor, not sitting somewhere else and contracting the labor out.

00:09:34.771 --> 00:09:37.288
<v Casey>Good place to start today's conversation.

00:09:38.688 --> 00:09:55.419
<v Riley>There's a question sitting underneath a lot of the agriculture news this week. When a government or a market decides an operation, a herd, a forest is worth saving, who's actually in the room making that call, and who's just living with the paperwork afterward?

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:10.553
<v Casey>The USDA gave us a live test case for exactly that. Secretary Brooke Rollins announced the Ranchers First Initiative — five actions meant to help rebuild the U.S. cattle herd, which is sitting at its lowest level in 75 years.

00:10:10.813 --> 00:10:27.545
<v Riley>And it's not a press release the industry is ignoring. R-CALF USA — a rancher advocacy group, not a government mouthpiece — called it "important steps" against what they describe as policy-driven forces suppressing marketplace competition and enabling foreign capture of the beef supply chain.

00:10:27.805 --> 00:10:38.588
<v Casey>Cautiously. Notice the hedge — "important steps," not "the fix." Producers quoted in RealAgriculture are a lot less generous. One rancher flat out called it "a nice fluff piece."

00:10:38.848 --> 00:10:59.298
<v Casey>Because the expanded heifer retention insurance still costs the rancher more money out of pocket every year, and the FSA loan requirements ask for cash flow proformas that don't match how a cattle operation's income actually lands through a calendar year. That's paperwork written like a tech startup's pitch deck, not a cattle ranch's ledger.

00:10:59.559 --> 00:11:17.034
<v Riley>Sure, nothing says "understands a drought summer" like a spreadsheet built for quarterly earnings calls. But that's a design flaw in the form, not proof the whole initiative is empty — R-CALF's endorsement means the underlying diagnosis is right, even if the loan paperwork itself needs fixing.

00:11:17.295 --> 00:11:30.308
<v Casey>Being right about the disease and being useless about the treatment are two different failures. The second one is the one that shows up on a rancher's balance sheet come renewal season, not the first.

00:11:30.569 --> 00:11:42.467
<v Riley>Cariboo ranching runs a version of the same math — commodity prices, drought, and now support programs designed somewhere else entirely. Whoever writes the eligibility criteria decides who qualifies and who doesn't.

00:11:42.727 --> 00:11:55.369
<v Casey>And it almost never gets debated out loud. Someone in a federal office designs the form, and it becomes the gate everyone downstream has to pass through, whether it fits their operation or not.

00:11:55.629 --> 00:12:12.733
<v Riley>Which is exactly why the Columbia Basin story sits well next to this one. Columbia Basin Trust is running two programs right now helping food producers install solar and trial new crops — reducing input costs and diversifying what actually grows there as weather patterns shift.

00:12:12.993 --> 00:12:22.289
<v Casey>That's a regional trust with local producers represented, not a federal agency writing loan criteria from an office two thousand miles from the nearest cow.

00:12:22.549 --> 00:12:38.166
<v Riley>A trust board with people who actually run operations in that basin designs support that fits the operations underneath it. A federal loan office designs support that fits its own audit requirements, and the rancher has to make the two things match.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:13:02.396
<v Casey>Which brings up the Bolivia story, because it's the sharper, higher-stakes version of the same problem. In Pando region, in the northern Bolivian Amazon, a 2024 court ruling opened the door to carbon credit deals on Indigenous land, and now companies are racing to sign contracts with communities before those communities fully understand what they're valuing, or what rights they might be signing away.

00:13:02.656 --> 00:13:18.273
<v Riley>A Mongabay investigation from last November documented one company, called Biota, locking in multiple carbon contracts plus a letter of intent with local nonprofits and a university, all in the same region, moving fast, ahead of any community-wide understanding of the terms.

00:13:18.533 --> 00:13:30.060
<v Casey>Leaders in Pando are publicly calling it a new form of extraction. Instead of monetizing timber or land the way earlier booms did, someone's now monetizing the air above the forest.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:35.154
<v Riley>Next someone will figure out how to put a price on the view.

00:13:35.414 --> 00:13:36.901
<v Casey>Don't give them ideas.

00:13:37.335 --> 00:14:05.593
<v Riley>It's not proof carbon markets themselves are a bad idea, though. It's a governance failure — thin legal capacity in the region, brokers moving faster than anyone can get a lawyer to actually read the contract. The fact that Pando's leaders are publicly denouncing these deals is itself a sign the 2001 collective title recognition still functions as a check. In an old-style extractive boom, there'd be no legal standing to object to anything at all.

00:14:05.854 --> 00:14:19.611
<v Casey>Having the legal standing to object after a deal's already signed isn't the same as having the leverage to negotiate before it. The contracts with Biota are already inked. Objection after the fact doesn't unwind the terms.

00:14:19.871 --> 00:14:28.795
<v Riley>That's an argument for building legal and technical capacity faster in these communities, not for treating carbon markets as inherently rigged against Indigenous landholders.

00:14:29.055 --> 00:14:52.108
<v Casey>It's an argument for asking who gets to write the valuation methodology before the money shows up at the village plaza. Nobody in Pando was in that room when the price of a ton of carbon got set — the identical problem to the FSA loan proformas, just with a longer legal history behind it, and higher stakes riding on the outcome.

00:14:52.802 --> 00:15:15.855
<v Riley>Which is exactly where Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation earns the comparison, and not just as a feel-good local example to end on. It's a joint venture — Tŝideldel and Tl'etinqox hold ownership themselves in the forest rehabilitation and biomass operation, with Yunesit'in territory part of the working area too. Nobody outside those Nations is writing the operating criteria for how that business runs.

00:15:16.116 --> 00:15:28.386
<v Casey>That's the structural difference that actually matters. Ownership sitting inside the community doing the labor, instead of a federal loan office or an outside carbon broker writing the terms and handing them down.

00:15:28.646 --> 00:15:47.237
<v Riley>And CCR has been running long enough — multiple fire seasons, not one grant cycle — to show the model holds up under real operating pressure. Compare that to Bolivia, where the carbon deals are months old and the terms are still opaque even to the people who signed them.

00:15:47.497 --> 00:16:06.088
<v Casey>Time in operation is one useful test. The other test is whether the terms were ever legible to the signatories in the first place. CCR passes both, because it's the Nations' own venture from the ground up. A carbon broker showing up in Pando with a template contract passes neither.

00:16:06.609 --> 00:16:25.200
<v Riley>The actual lever, then, is who's holding the pen when the eligibility rules get written in the first place — a trust board with local seats and local producers, a Nation-owned venture, versus a federal agency or an outside broker working off a template built for a different operation entirely.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:51.860
<v Casey>That's the honest split between these stories. Ranchers First shows what happens when the pen is federal and the form doesn't fit the operation. Columbia Basin Trust and Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation show what changes when the pen moves closer to the ground it's governing. Whether that scales up to something the size of rebuilding the entire U.S. cattle herd is a much harder question, and nothing in today's material answers it.

00:16:52.120 --> 00:17:07.365
<v Riley>Worth leaving open rather than forcing it. It might be a scale problem more than a pure governance problem — a regional trust works partly because the region is small enough that everyone affected can actually sit at the same table.

00:17:07.625 --> 00:17:12.830
<v Casey>Which is its own limit worth naming honestly, not a solution to wave off.

00:17:13.525 --> 00:17:33.975
<v Riley>If there's a concrete thing worth trying here, it's smaller than launching a new program from scratch. A Cariboo producer association could push to review loan criteria or cost-share terms before they're finalized, not after they've already been printed. Columbia Basin Trust's structure is a working template for what that review could look like locally.

00:17:34.235 --> 00:17:48.736
<v Casey>And whoever ends up drafting carbon offset language for Cariboo forest tenures should be asking the Pando question up front — who in the room actually understands the contract, and who just showed up needing the cheque that week.

00:17:49.170 --> 00:18:06.645
<v Riley>If you've got a story about who's actually writing the rules for working lands programs around here — a producer association, a Nation-led venture, anyone with a seat at the table when the terms get set — send it our way. Feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:18:06.906 --> 00:18:09.508
<v Casey>We'll take it. Have a good one.

00:18:09.769 --> 00:18:12.372
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day.